      "writtenOn": "2021-09-19T03:55:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think these semantics make sense.\n\n- This is some triple negative logic : it\u0027s rematch all, but it\u0027s disabled by default which means the feature is enabled, but if the flag is off the disabling is disabled, which is what we want, unless the module is too old, in which case it disables the disabling of the disabling.\n- If the module is too old, it gets the new behavior. (??)\n- Then you can argue that older modules don\u0027t even have that code, so they still don\u0027t have the new behavior. The behavior is the opposite of what the comments say !\n- We can set the flag to be disabled starting at some module version, but not enabled. But that is the opposite of how development happens ! Suppose there is a bug in the current code (version N) and we find it later and fix it in version N+1 : we\u0027ll want to enable the new behavior in version N+1 and disable it in version N. But these semantics are set up so we exactly can\u0027t do that ! If we actually need to use the kill switch we\u0027ll need this logic the other way around.\n\nI get that you want to be able to eventually remove the flag from the server-side config, but not even using some super contrived logic like this achieves this : if we need to use the kill switch, we\u0027ll need to use it forever.\n\nGiven that the general logic for flags is \"enabled from this version\" anyway, can\u0027t we have kill switches test use the default value for the version int instead of the default value for the flag boolean ?\nE.g. when everything goes well and we delete the flag, the device sees no value for the kill switch, so it thinks the behavior is enabled from version 0, so it says true.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 27,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "84d82d013f709a02ecb62c8e472ef3cbe37635bb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
